# Product thesis

We are building a NumPy-feel image compute graph for 2D spatial pipelines
(RAW / DNG first, imaging-general after). It sits next to NumPy and
OpenCV; it does not replace them.

Constraints when planning or changing API, scheduling, or kernels:

- The API design defaults to "match numpy exactly", only diverge from that 
  in exceptional cases.
- Operators build a lazy graph. Full-frame work happens on `.realize()`.
- Prefer a span implementation of an operator (it processes one row
  span at a time), then a tiled one. A full-frame implementation is
  the last option. 
- Operator fusion is an engine execute step. The public catalog stays small
  functional units (`rgb_matrix_3x3`, `lut`, …), not opaque composed ops.
- The Python surface stays ordinary array code (slice, astype, `*`),
  not a scheduling language.
- `mi.Array` is a handle, not “a NumPy array.” Today the backing is
  ndarray; leave room for other handles (native buffer, JAX, torch).
  Dropping to NumPy / OpenCV via `.realize()` / `__array__` stays
  effortless. Do not wall those libraries off.
- Every op has a float32 path. Lower precision is extra, not a
  replacement.
- Array-valued parameters (matrix, lut, gain) may later be Array
  inputs, not only frozen attrs. Control flags (dtype name, `fast`,
  pad mode) stay attrs. Do not harden “exactly one image input.”
- Do not add a compiler or JIT toolchain as a required dependency.
- Ship a small native CPU catalog as the default. Optional JAX,
  Numba, or Halide backends consume the same IR; they do not replace
  the lightweight kernels.

Public spelling: `import muimage as mi`, `mi.Array`, `.realize()`.
Today the `muimage` top-level package ships from the muraw wheel.
Call sites keep that import when the graph is extracted.

This is the destination. Do not rewrite working eager or unfused code
to match the thesis unless the task is that change.
